To determine when two lines are coplanar, we can use the following approach:

1. The two lines are coplanar if the vector joining a point on one line to a point on the other line is perpendicular to the cross product of the direction vectors of the two lines.

2. Let’s denote the direction vectors of the lines as $\mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ k \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{v}_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 4 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}$. Let’s also denote the vector joining a point on Line 1 to a point on Line 2 as $\mathbf{w} = \mathbf{r}_2(0) - \mathbf{r}_1(0)$, where $\mathbf{r}_1(0)$ is the point on Line 1 when $t = 0$ (i.e., $\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -3 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix}$) and $\mathbf{r}_2(0)$ is the point on Line 2 when $u = 0$ (i.e., $\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 4 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}$).

3. Therefore, $\mathbf{w} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 4 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -3 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 7 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix}$.

